WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:12.209
Today we are going to see how a robot doggy
can help us explore dangerous areas without

00:00:12.209 --> 00:00:14.919
putting humans at risk.

00:00:14.919 --> 00:00:21.080
Well, not this one, mind you, because this
is not performing too well.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:22.690
Why is that?

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:26.060
Well, this is a proprioceptive robot.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:29.460
This means that the robot is essentially blind.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.539
Yes.

00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:31.619
Really.

00:00:31.619 --> 00:00:36.010
It only senses its own internal state and
that’s it.

00:00:36.010 --> 00:00:42.000
For instance, it knows about its orientation
and twist of the base unit, a little joint

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.180
information, like positions and velocities,
and that’s about it.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:51.039
It is still remarkable that it can navigate
at all.

00:00:51.039 --> 00:00:57.359
However, this often means that the robot has
to feel out the terrain before being able

00:00:57.359 --> 00:00:59.239
to navigate on it.

00:00:59.239 --> 00:01:00.739
Why would we do that?

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:07.790
Well, if we have difficult seeing conditions,
for instance, dust, fog, smoke, a robot that

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:12.380
does not rely on seeing is suddenly super
useful.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:18.640
However, when we have good seeing conditions,
as you see, we have to be able to do better

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.070
than this.

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:21.780
So, can we?

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:24.970
Well, have a look at this new technique.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:27.189
Well, now we’re talking!

00:01:27.189 --> 00:01:34.549
This new robot is exteroceptive, which means
that it has cameras, it can see, but it also

00:01:34.549 --> 00:01:41.770
has proprioceptive sensors too, these are
the ones that tell the orientation and similar

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:42.770
information.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:49.469
But, this new one fuses together proprioception
and exteroception.

00:01:49.469 --> 00:01:53.259
What does that mean?

00:01:53.259 --> 00:01:58.700
The best of both worlds!

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:01.510
Here you see how it sees the world.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:07.320
Thus, it does not need to feel out the terrain
before the navigation, but, and here is the

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:08.320
key.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:14.520
Now, hold on to your papers, because it can
even navigate reasonably well even if its

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:18.870
sensors get covered.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.010
Look.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.360
That is absolutely amazing.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:30.650
And, with the covers removed, we can give
it another try, let’s see the difference…and,

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:32.680
oh yeah!

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.280
Back in the game baby!

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:36.720
So far, great news!

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:39.890
But, what are the limits of this machine?

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:41.330
Can it climb stairs?

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:45.200
Well, let’s have a look.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:47.230
Yup.

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:48.590
Not a problem.

00:02:48.590 --> 00:02:55.150
Not only that, but it can even go on a long
hike in Switzerland and reach the summit without

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:56.600
any issues.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:02.130
And in general, it can navigate a variety
of really difficult terrains.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:11.660
So, if it can do all that, now, let’s be
really tough on it, and put it to the real

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:12.800
test.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:20.700
This is the testing footage before the grand
challenge.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:22.760
Flying colors.

00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:28.570
So, let’s see how it did in the grand challenge
itself.

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:30.780
Oh my.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:37.650
You see, here, it has to navigate an underground
environment completely autonomously.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.520
This really is the ultimate test.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:46.370
No help is allowed, it has to figure out everything
by itself.

00:03:46.370 --> 00:03:52.840
This test has uneven terrain with lots of
rubble, difficult seeing conditions, dust,

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:55.560
tunnels, caves, you name it.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.950
An absolute nightmare scenario.

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:00.510
And, this is incredible.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:06.740
During this challenge, it explored more than
a mile, and how many times has it fallen?

00:04:06.740 --> 00:04:08.850
Well, what do you think?

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:09.850
Zero.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:10.850
Yes.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:12.700
Zero times.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.250
Wow.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:16.500
Absolutely amazing.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:22.030
While we look at how well it can navigate
these super slippery and squishy terrains,

00:04:22.030 --> 00:04:26.840
let’s ask the key question: what is all
this good for?

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:32.700
Well, chiefly, for exploring under explored
and dangerous areas.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:38.630
This means tons of useful applications, for
instance, a variant of this this could help

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:45.680
save humans stuck under rubble, or perhaps
even explore other planets without putting

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:48.010
humans at risk.

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:49.010
And more.

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:51.160
And, what do you think?

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:53.120
What would you use this for?

00:04:53.120 --> 00:05:53.120
Let me know in the comments below!

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.370
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

